# Testing & verify flows

Each repository documents **concrete paths, config file names, and run
commands** in **`tests/README.md`**. Maintain **`docs/specification/as-built/`**
as the SSOT for layout and CI vs live boundaries.

## Objectives

- Prove reconcile + scrape + buffer + forward without requiring physical
  hardware in CI.
- Separate **fast in-process pytest** from **compose verify** from
  **on-device** checks.
- Keep secrets and machine-specific URLs out of version control.

## Standard folder layout

```text
tests/
  test_*.py               # pytest — in-process (may use tests/unit/ later)
  verify/                 # docker-compose broker + fake/real local engine
    verify_*.py
  device/                 # real edge hardware — documented, not CI-run
  debug/                  # exploration — not gating
  _helpers/               # shared — not collected by pytest
  config.yaml.example
  config.yaml             # local only (gitignored)
```

## Categories

| Category | Role | Typical invocation |
|----------|------|--------------------|
| **Pytest** | Fast in-process; mock MQTT, container runtime, hardware telemetry | `make test` |
| **Verify** | Contract smoke against compose stack (broker + engine stand-in) | `python -m tests.verify.<script>` |
| **Device** | Real SoC / GPU / slot behaviour | Documented in `tests/README.md`; **not** CI |
| **Debug** | Exploration | `python -m tests.debug.<script>` |

**`make test`** must ignore `tests/verify/`, `tests/device/`, `tests/debug/`,
and `tests/e2e/`.

## CI vs live

| Layer | Typical CI | Local / board Verify |
|-------|------------|----------------------|
| `make check` | yes | yes |
| `make test` | yes | yes |
| `tests/verify/*` | optional if compose available | yes |
| `tests/device/*` | **no** | on hardware only |

## Practices

- Exit `0` on success, non-zero on failure for verify/device scripts.
- Prefer enums from **`src/models/`** in assertions — no magic milestone strings.
- Do not duplicate full compose journeys in pytest when verify already covers them;
  use pytest for logic branches with mocks.

